# villageHall — Órlaith at the hearth
#
# First speaking part inside the chieftain's hall. Deliberately short and
# mostly linear -- this is a mood-setting/transition beat, not a branching
# interrogation like Muireann or Odhrán. The three side-questions are
# optional flavour; "Rest by the fire" is the one that matters and is
# always available, so a player who wants to move straight through can.
#
# Fionnbarra is NOT present -- explicit design call. Órlaith is a
# reachtaire (household steward, real historical term: "controller of a
# royal household, administrator"), running the hall in his absence.
#
# q_baile completes HERE (resting by the fire), not on arriving at the
# outer rath -- moved from b0.js per discussion.
#
# has_brat: the note representing the mantle she drapes over the player.
# Same pattern as has_toll_rope in d3.dlg (a note standing in for an
# item, not a full inventory system, since none exists yet).
#
# WHAT HAPPENS AFTER "Rest by the fire" IS DELIBERATE, HERE: the JS side
# (villageHall.js) watches for has_brat and fades the screen to black once
# it's set -- no dream content, no wake-up beat. That's chapter 2, held
# back on purpose. This is the end of chapter 1's content, not a bug.

@file village/villageHall


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 0 — the greeting
@continue
Ah -- another one, blown in off the river.
% Á, ceann eile, séidte isteach ón abhainn.

Well. Come to the fire. Custom asks nothing of a guest,
% Bhuel. Tar chun na tine. Ní iarrann an nós faic ar aoi,

so I'll ask nothing either -- for now.
% mar sin ní iarrfaidh mise faic ort -- go fóill.


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 1 — the hearth
@hold
@again Sit when you're ready. / Suigh nuair is mian leat.
Sit, if the legs will let you.
% Suigh, más féidir le do chosa é.

There's little enough company here tonight to mind you sitting.
% Is beag an chuideachta atá anseo anocht le do shuí a thabhairt faoi deara.

* Who are you?
% Cé thusa?
@when !note knows_orlaith
@note knows_orlaith
> Who are you?
% Cé thusa?
< Órlaith, reachtaire of this house.
% Órlaith, reachtaire an tí seo.
< I keep it when himself does not -- which, of late, is most nights.
% Coinním é nuair nach ndéanann sé féin -- rud, le déanaí, is mó ná a mhalairt.

* Where is everyone?
% Cá bhfuil chuile dhuine?
@when !note heard_hall_is_quiet
@note heard_hall_is_quiet
> Where is everyone?
% Cá bhfuil chuile dhuine?
< Sour word came ahead of you.
% Tháinig scéal searbh romhat.
< Folk keep to their own hearths till the air clears.
% Coinníonn daoine lena dteallach féin go nglanann an t-aer.

* Is the chieftain here?
% An bhfuil an Taoiseach anseo?
@when !note knows_fionnbarra_absent
@note knows_fionnbarra_absent
> Is the chieftain here?
% An bhfuil an Taoiseach anseo?
< He is not.
% Níl.
< Nor will he be, tonight.
% Ná ní bheidh, anocht.

* Rest by the fire
% Lig do scíth cois na tine
@done q_baile
@note has_brat
> I'll rest, if I may.
% Ligfidh mé mo scíth, más é do thoil é.
< Seven days you've been on that river, by the look of you.
% Seacht lá atá caite agat ar an abhainn sin, de réir do chuma.
< Rest the limbs. You're not where you meant to be, but you're safe.
% Lig do do ghéaga. Níl tú san áit a raibh tú ag dul, ach tá tú slán.
< The fire is warm, and the pot smells better than it looks.
% Tá an tine te, agus tá boladh níos fearr ón bpota ná mar a fheictear air.
< Someone drapes a heavy brat over your shoulders as your eyes close.
% Sínítear brat trom thar do ghuaillí agus do shúile ag dúnadh.

* Leave
% Fág
@exit
@silent
